{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST parse Rust, Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML, and YAML\nsources with pinned tree-sitter grammars, selected by file extension. Every\nother text file MUST use a plain-text profile in which each line that is not\nblank is an anonymous outline node of kind `line`. Each supported language MUST\nhave an outline profile that maps grammar node kinds to short outline kind names\nand, for each mapped kind, states whether a node is named, and by which fields\nor children, or anonymous. Only mapped kinds MUST appear in the outline; other\nnodes MUST be transparent, so their outline descendants appear under the\nnearest outline ancestor. A profile MAY attach grammar siblings to an outline\nnode, such as Rust attributes and doc comments preceding an item, so the outline\ntree need not match the grammar tree exactly; attached siblings are part of that\nnode for its line range and hash. Every file's outline MUST have a root node\nfor the whole file, independent of language. Source in no other outline node,\nsuch as top-level statements no profile maps, MUST belong to the root node;\nprofiles MUST NOT invent fallback kinds for it. Comments MUST NOT be outline nodes. Profiles MUST be\ndocumented. Upgrading a grammar or profile MAY change outlines and MUST be\ntreated as a deliberate change.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: false.** The core of the requirement is implemented, tested and documented. The gap is that some code marked `{impl}` for it implements behaviour it does not specify.\n\n**What is satisfied**\n- **Pinned grammars.** `Cargo.toml` pins `tree-sitter` and every grammar crate with `=x.y.z`.\n- **Profiles by extension.** `profiles()` and `profile_for_path` in `src/outline/profile.rs` cover Rust, Python, TypeScript (plus TSX), CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML, selected by extension. Everything else falls back to the plain-text profile (`src/outline/plain.rs`).\n- **Plain text.** `plain_text` in `src/outline/mod.rs` makes each non-blank line an anonymous `line` node.\n- **Profile tables.** Each profile is a table of `KindRule` rows (grammar kind, short kind, `NameRule`). `NameRule` expresses anonymous, by field, by first child, or custom naming.\n- **Transparency, root, comments.** `Builder::walk` treats unmapped nodes as transparent. `root_node` always creates a root. Source in no mapped node stays with the root; the `Builder::walk` comment says so, and no fallback kinds are invented. `Builder::maps` excludes comments from the outline. Rust attributes and doc comments, and Python decorators, attach to the following item via the `attaches` hooks. `finish` includes the attached siblings in the line range.\n- **Documentation.** `md/reference.md` has an \"Outlines\" section with a profile table, per-profile details, and a statement that grammar versions are pinned and that upgrades are deliberate changes.\n- **Tests.** `src/outline/tests.rs` has a test per profile (Python, TypeScript/TSX, CSS, Markdown, TOML/YAML), plain-text lines, parse failures, root ownership, and `no_profile_invents_fallback_kinds`. The `outline` tool test in `src/authoring/tools.rs` also references the requirement. Coverage of the design space is good.\n\n**Gaps**\n1. **Line queries.** Several functions marked `{impl}` for this requirement alone implement behaviour the requirement does not specify:\n   - `Outline::innermost_at_line`\n   - `Outline::declared_on_line` (named nodes on the line of their name, anonymous nodes on their start line)\n   - `Outline::node_lines`\n   - `OutlineNode::display_name` (joins name parts with `/`)\n\n   `declared_on_line` is used by `add_input.rs`, and `node_lines` by `inputs.rs`. Neither carries an `{impl}` marker for a definition that specifies that behaviour. The other line-query helpers (`nodes_for_lines`, `owner_at_line`) do carry references to other requirements. The test `line_queries_find_innermost_and_declared_nodes` cites `selector_same_line`, but the code under test does not carry that reference.\n2. **Parse-error and extension behaviour.** `check_errors` rejects any file with a syntax error and reports the first error's line, wrapped by `OutlineError`'s `Display` (\"line N: …\"). `profile_for_path` matches extensions case-insensitively. The requirement does not say either of these. The reference text only mentions an unparseable Rust file, under a different requirement.\n\nNo FIXME acknowledges these gaps.",
